    <string name="fusion_recipe_pp_science">In stellar cores, 4 protons fuse via quantum tunneling into helium-4, powering stars for billions of years.</string>
    <string name="fusion_recipe_triple_alpha_science">Three helium-4 nuclei collide through the Hoyle resonance to forge a carbon-12 nucleus.</string>
    <string name="fusion_recipe_alpha_capture_science">Two neon-20 nuclei undergo photodisintegration in a stellar core, releasing oxygen-16 and energetic alpha particles.</string>
    <string name="fusion_recipe_carbon_fusion_science">Two carbon-12 nuclei merge in the core of a massive star, yielding neon-20.</string>
    <string name="fusion_recipe_oxygen_fusion_science">Two oxygen-16 nuclei fuse in a star\'s core, producing sulfur-32.</string>
    <string name="fusion_recipe_water_science">Through successive fusion reactions, sulfur nuclei are consumed in a massive star\'s core, ultimately forging iron-56 — the endpoint of stellar fusion.</string>
